    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you go into a bit more detail on when we'd choose one over the other? Like what are the main trade-offs in terms of cost and complexity? I want to make sure they understand why we can't just pick one and stick to it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When deciding between vertical and horizontal scaling, the main trade-offs are costs and complexity. Vertical scaling is often more cost-effective, as you're upgrading existing resources without adding new infrastructure, but it can become increasingly inefficient and expensive as your workload grows. In contrast, horizontal scaling is more flexible and scalable, but it can be more complex to manage and can incur additional costs for new infrastructure, instance provisioning, and monitoring. For example, if you're processing high-frequency trades, vertical scaling might be sufficient, but if you're expecting exponential growth in usage, horizontal scaling would be a better fit.",
      "source": "target",
